course: dictionary
lesson: 7106
title: Peasant: one word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Peasant is one of them. Use the clues in the sentence to find out which meaning the writer means. «In 1381 English peasants angry about taxes and unfair laws marched on London.» The taxes and the long-ago date tell you these peasants were poor farm workers in the past.
explain: Today, a peasant can also be a farmer on a small farm who grows food mostly for the family. The insult is different. If someone calls you a peasant, they mean you are uneducated or have bad manners. That is unkind, so do not use it to hurt people.
explain: Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it. «Peasant farmers» on a small family farm makes sense. Calling someone a peasant as an insult makes sense only when the sentence is about bad manners, not about land or taxes.
fact: A «peasant» was a poor farmer or farm worker in the past, especially in the Middle Ages.
fact: Peasants often worked a small piece of land and had to give part of their crops to a lord.
fact: In history lessons, peasants are also called serfs or villeins.
fact: «Peasant» can be a rude insult, meaning someone is uneducated or has bad manners. It is unkind.
fact: «Peasant farmers» still grow food mostly for their families in some countries today.
exercise: «In 1381 English peasants angry about taxes marched on London.» Who were the peasants in this sentence? | *Poor farm workers from long ago | Kings and lords with big castles | Sailors on the sea | Knights in shining armour
exercise: Someone calls Sam a peasant because he chews with his mouth open. What does the word mean here? | *An unkind insult meaning bad manners | A word for a king | A kind compliment about his farm | It means he is very tall
exercise: Which fact is true about a peasant in the past? | He was a king who ruled the land | *He had to give part of his crops to a lord | He never worked on the land | He lived in a castle with knights
test: In fairy tales, «a clever peasant often tricks a king.» Who is the clever one? | The baron | *The peasant | The knight | The king
test: Which is a peasant farmer today? | A shop owner in a big town | *A farmer on a small farm who grows food mostly for the family | A sailor on a ship | A ruler who owns a huge land
exam: Which meaning fits the rude use of peasant? | A poor farm worker in the Middle Ages | *Uneducated, with bad manners, and unkind | A knight who fights for a king | A small farm that grows food for the family
